{
  "passages": [
    {
      "reference": "16",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": false,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": false,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": false
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "τὸ σὲ οἰκονομεῖν ἀναδέξασθαι χάριν οἶδα εἰληφώς, οὐ δεδωκώς",
          "english_quote": "in undertaking the task of having you administer, I know that I have received a favor, not given one.",
          "issue": "The draft makes the speaker undertake the task of having Clement administer. A more direct construal takes σέ as the subject of ἀναδέξασθαι, with οἰκονομεῖν as its complement: Clement undertakes to administer, and the speaker thereby receives a favor. The existing source_issues note acknowledges multiple construals but does not explain this competing analysis. This is an interpretive problem, not evidence of corrupt print.",
          "suggested_revision": "Candidate for fresh review: “Hence I know that, in your undertaking to administer, I have received a favor, not given one.” Revise the note to explain both nested-infinitive analyses rather than presenting only the speaker-as-subject interpretation.",
          "severity": "major"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "ἐκ χριστοῦ",
          "english_quote": "from Christ",
          "issue": "χριστοῦ functions independently as a title here, not within a personal-name construction. The supplied POB policy therefore calls for “Messiah.”",
          "suggested_revision": "Replace “from Christ” with “from the Messiah.”",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "θηρεύειν ἔπαινον",
          "english_quote": "pursue the beneficial praise",
          "issue": "θηρεύειν explicitly evokes hunting. “Pursue” weakens that image in the main translation, although the lexical note recognizes it. The source-transparent candidate “hunt for” preserves the author's metaphor without changing the object being sought.",
          "suggested_revision": "Use “hunt for the beneficial praise that comes from the Messiah,” retaining the contrast with shaking off praise arising from injustice.",
          "severity": "minor"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "reference": "17",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": true,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": false,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": true
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "χριστόν, οὗ τὴν καθέδραν πεπίστευται, οὐκ ἐδέξατο, καὶ ’ὡς χριστὸν μὴ δεξάμενος",
          "english_quote": "has not received Christ, whose chair has been entrusted to him; and as one who has not received Christ",
          "issue": "Both occurrences of χριστός are independently titular. Neither is part of a personal-name construction that would support the policy exception “Christ.”",
          "suggested_revision": "Use “has not received the Messiah, whose chair has been entrusted to him; and as one who has not received the Messiah.” Preserve both negations and the entrusted-chair image.",
          "severity": "minor"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "reference": "18",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": false,
        "no_added_claims": true,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": false,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": true
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "ἐπιδικαζόμενος τοῦ πάντας φίλους ἔχειν",
          "english_quote": "seeking to have everyone as friends",
          "issue": "The middle participle carries a claim-pressing or assertion nuance that generic “seeking” does not express. The lexical note recognizes this nuance but does not preserve it in the translation. Recovering it does not resolve the separately disclosed uncertainty about the pronoun antecedents.",
          "suggested_revision": "Compare “pressing his claim to have everyone as friends” as the more source-transparent candidate. Retain the existing uncertainty concerning “him” and “his discourse”; do not identify their antecedents without further argument.",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "τῶν ἔξωθεν φαινομένων ἐχθρῶν",
          "english_quote": "the enemies who appear openly",
          "issue": "The draft omits ἔξωθεν, “from outside.” Openness does not convey external location or affiliation. The omission also obscures the deliberate contrast with τοὺς ἔσω, “those within,” at the end of the sentence.",
          "suggested_revision": "Use “the obvious enemies from outside,” or another rendering that explicitly preserves both their manifest enmity and their position outside.",
          "severity": "major"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "reference": "19",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": true,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": false,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": false
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "σὲ Ἰακώβῳ τῷ ἀδελφῷ τοῦ κυρίου διαπέμψαι ἐν ἐπιτομῇ ἀναγραψάμενον",
          "english_quote": "After the earlier Ἀξιῶ σε, the second accusative σέ creates an irregular construction and could literally suggest sending Clement himself to James.",
          "issue": "The source_issues note misdiagnoses the accusative. σέ can resume the earlier σε as the accusative subject of διαπέμψαι, and ἀναγραψάμενον agrees with that subject. It therefore need not designate Clement as the object being sent. The larger sentence mixes constructions around ὅπως and μὴ ὀκνήσῃς, but that does not establish corruption of σέ or justify the proposed σύ.",
          "suggested_revision": "Retain the supplied σέ and remove the proposed correction σὺ Ἰακώβῳ. Explain the resumptive accusative and participial agreement. Any remaining difficulty in the extended sentence should be classified as syntactic or interpretive uncertainty, not as an established defective reading. The main rendering “send James ... a concise written account” does not require replacement on this ground.",
          "severity": "major"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "ὃ πάντως ἔδει με παθεῖν εὐσεβῶς ἀπεδόμην",
          "english_quote": "I have piously paid what I was in every way bound to suffer.",
          "issue": "In this construction πάντως reinforces the certainty or inevitability of the necessary suffering. “In every way” instead suggests necessity in every respect or manner, weakening the connection with the unavoidable death being discussed.",
          "suggested_revision": "Replace the relevant phrase with “I have piously paid what I was inevitably bound to suffer.” Retain the source's obligation/payment language rather than introducing a different account of the death.",
          "severity": "minor"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "reference": "20",
      "verdict": "accept",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": true,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": true,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": true
      },
      "findings": []
    }
  ]
}
